  "realisation": "implicit_trunk_over_pelvis",
  "realisation_note": "SS and PT are the pelvis' orientation against the vertical. With the pelvis held, the only image-space trace of a different standing PT* is the orientation of the trunk relative to the sacrum, which is the sum of the segmental angles: d(trunk vs sacrum) = dLL - dTK. So the pelvic-tilt compensation is realised by the segmental deltas themselves and by nothing else; a separate rigid rotation of the column on the sacrum would double-count it and would change the measured LL by the same amount. The task text's 'junction rotation IS the pelvic-tilt compensation' is honoured in this sense: the junction carries its share of dLL (zero when fused) and the drawn PT* is the standing tilt that the resulting relative geometry corresponds to, recorded in the manifest, checked through the balance gate, never applied as a rotation",
